+# SPEC: CSV import of contacts
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and adds them to contactbook one at a time. They
+want to load a whole CSV file in one step and get a report of what happened: which
+contacts were created, which were updated, which rows were rejected and why, and
+anything that was noticed but did not block a row. The file uses the same columns as
+our CSV export (`export.py`). Files written by our address-book export script are
+also accepted; that script writes `first_name`/`last_name` instead of `name`.
+
+The import adds to the data and never takes anything away. A spreadsheet is not the
+full record of a contact, so an import never removes tags and never wipes a phone
+number. Bad rows are skipped and reported, and the good rows still go in. A file
+that can't be imported as a whole is rejected without changing anything, so a
+contact book is never left half-loaded.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number**: the 1-based line number in the file, the same number a spreadsheet
+  shows. The header is row 1 and the line after it is row 2. Every line counts,
+  blank lines included. A record whose quoted cell spans several lines is numbered by
+  the line it starts on.
+- **Blank row**: an empty line, or a line where every cell is empty or only
+  whitespace (for example Excel's `,,,` lines).
+- **Data row**: any row after the header that is not blank.
+- **Canonical column**: one of `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`. Every `RowIssue.column`
+  is one of these four or `None`.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Input and encoding
+
+1. `bytes` input is decoded as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM is accepted and ignored.
+2. `str` input is accepted as is. A leading BOM character (`﻿`) is ignored, the
+   same as for bytes.
+3. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`. The message tells the user
+   to re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8". There is no fallback to another encoding.
+4. Lines may end in `\n` or `\r\n`. A trailing line break at the end of the file
+   does not create an extra row.
+5. The delimiter is the comma. A `;`-delimited file is not specially detected. It
+   normally fails criterion 8, because its header doesn't contain the required columns.
+6. Passing anything other than `str` or `bytes` as `data` raises `TypeError`, not
+   `ImportFileError`. Programming errors are not wrapped (see `errors.py`).
+
+### Header
+
+7. The first line of the file is the header. Header names are matched ignoring case
+   and surrounding whitespace (`" Email "` matches `email`), and column order is free.
+8. The file must contain an `email` column and a name source. A name source is either
+   a `name` column or **both** `first_name` and `last_name` columns. Otherwise
+   `ImportFileError` is raised, and the message names what is missing.
+   - If `name` and the `first_name`/`last_name` pair are both present, `name` is used,
+     and `first_name`/`last_name` are treated as unrecognised columns.
+   - `first_name` without `last_name` (or the other way round) and without `name`
+     counts as no name source.
+9. `phone` and `tags` are optional columns.
+10. If a recognised column (`name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, `first_name`,
+    `last_name`) appears more than once after case/whitespace matching, the file
+    raises `ImportFileError`. Repeated unrecognised or empty header cells are not an
+    error.
+11. Unrecognised columns (for example `company`) are ignored and are never an error.
+    Each one may produce a warning with `row=1` and `column=None`.
+
+### Whole-file rejection
+
+12. These raise `ImportFileError`:
+    - an empty file, or one containing only whitespace
+    - invalid UTF-8 (criterion 3)
+    - a missing required column (criterion 8)
+    - a duplicated recognised column (criterion 10)
+    - CSV structure that can't be parsed, for example a quoted cell that is never closed
+    - more than 1000 data rows (criterion 13)
+13. The limit is at most 1000 data rows per file. Blank rows don't count. Data rows
+    that end up as errors or duplicates do count. A file with exactly 1000 data rows
+    is imported. A file with 1001 is rejected.
+14. Whenever `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is exactly as it was before
+    the call: no contact is added or changed. This holds even when the problem is
+    found near the end of the file.
+15. A file with a valid header and no data rows (only the header, or the header plus
+    blank rows) is not an error. It returns an empty report and leaves the repository
+    unchanged.
+
+### Row handling
+
+16. Blank rows are skipped silently. They produce no issue, but they still count in
+    the row numbering.
+17. A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as empty, and the
+    normal rules then apply. A row with more cells than the header ignores the extra
+    cells and records a warning for that row with `column=None`.
+18. **Email** is normalised with the existing email rules (trimmed, lower-cased,
+    validated). A missing or invalid email makes the row an error with
+    `column="email"`.
+19. **Name** comes from the `name` cell. In a `first_name`/`last_name` file it is
+    built by joining the non-blank parts with one space (only `last_name="Lee"`
+    gives `"Lee"`). The result then goes through the existing name rules: trimmed,
+    inner whitespace collapsed, and at most 100 characters. A blank or invalid name
+    makes the row an error with `column="name"`, also in a `first_name`/`last_name`
+    file. Name is required on every row, including rows that update an existing
+    contact.
+20. **Tags**: the cell is split on `;`. Each piece is trimmed, empty pieces are
+    ignored, and each tag is normalised with the existing tag rules (so `"Key Account"`
+    becomes `key-account`). Repeats collapse. If any tag is invalid, the row is an
+    error with `column="tags"`. An empty tags cell, or no tags column, means no tags
+    from this row.
+21. **Phone**: a blank phone cell, or no phone column, means no phone from this row.
+    A non-blank phone is normalised with the existing phone rules. If it fails
+    validation, the row is **not** an error: it is still applied without the phone,
+    and a warning is recorded with `column="phone"`.
+22. A row with at least one error is not applied at all: no contact is created or
+    changed by it. It produces at least one entry in `errors` with that row's row
+    number. Whether a row with several problems produces one entry or several is not
+    specified.
+23. Errors in one row don't affect other rows. Every valid row in the file is applied,
+    whatever errors appear before or after it.
+
+### Creating and updating
+
+24. Existing contacts are matched by normalised email (ignoring case), the same way
+    the repository looks them up.
+25. If a valid row's email is not in the repository, a contact is created with the
+    row's name, its tags, and its phone (or none, per criterion 21). The contact's
+    normalised email is appended to `created`.
+26. If a valid row's email already exists, the stored contact is updated as follows:
+    - **name** is replaced by the row's name
+    - **phone** is replaced only when the row has a valid non-blank phone. Otherwise
+      the existing phone is kept, so an import never clears a phone.
+    - **tags** become the union of the existing tags and the row's tags, so an import
+      never removes a tag.
+
+    If this changes the contact, its normalised email is appended to `updated`. If
+    the contact ends up identical to what was stored, the email goes in neither
+    `created` nor `updated`.
+27. **Duplicate emails within the file**: the first data row with a given normalised
+    email decides what happens. Every later data row with the same normalised email
+    is an error on that later row, with `column="email"`, and is not applied. The
+    first occurrence wins even if it was itself an error row. In that case the later
+    rows are still duplicates.
+28. Stored contacts hold only normalised values, the same values `Contact.create`
+    would produce.
+
+### Report
+
+29. `created` and `updated` list normalised emails in file order. No email appears
+    more than once across the two lists.
+30. `errors` and `warnings` are in row order. Each `RowIssue.message` is a short,
+    human-readable reason.
+31. Every `RowIssue.column` is `"name"`, `"email"`, `"phone"`, `"tags"` or `None`.
+    It is never a header spelling taken from the file, and never `first_name` or
+    `last_name`.
+
+### Round trip
+
+32. Importing the output of `export_csv(contacts)` into an empty repository creates
+    contacts equal to `contacts`, with no errors and no warnings.
+33. Importing `export_csv(repo.all())` back into the same `repo` reports nothing
+    created, nothing updated, and no errors, and it leaves the repository unchanged.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Encodings other than UTF-8 (no Windows-1252 or Latin-1 detection or guessing).
+- Delimiters other than the comma (no `;`-delimited files and no delimiter sniffing).
+- Removing anything through an import: deleting contacts, removing tags, or clearing
+  phones.
+- Files over 1000 data rows. Users split them. There is no streaming or batching.
+- Partial import of a file that has a whole-file problem.
+- A dry-run or preview mode, or a CLI or HTTP endpoint for the import. Those live in
+  other packages.
+- Changes to `export_csv` or its columns, including exporting `first_name`/`last_name`.
+- Thread safety. The repository isn't thread-safe either.
+- Mapping arbitrary column names (`E-mail`, `Full Name`, …) beyond the names listed above.
